## The Numbers Don't Lie: A Rising Tide of Recalls

With 577 food recalls reported so far this year, we are pacing towards yet another grim milestone. Among these, Class I recalls, deemed the most severe due to their potential for serious health consequences, stand at 176 cases. These aren't just numbers--they're signs of a systemic issue in the food safety chain.

## Galaxy RG Corp's Colorful Oversight

Now, let's talk cakes. Galaxy RG Corp is at the center of a recent recall involving 11 products, ranging from Cheesecake de Guava to Marquesita de Mango. These products were found to contain undeclared artificial colors, including Yellow #5, Yellow #6, and Red #40. While these colors are FDA-approved, undeclared allergens remain a significant concern for sensitive consumers. If you're one to scan ingredient labels for additives to avoid, this recall might be a call to double-check when you're indulging in a sweet treat.

## The Supplement Scare

But the recalls don't stop at cakes. Total Nutrition Inc also issued a Class I recall for its "Complete Green Superfood Moringa Capsules," citing potential Salmonella contamination. Unlike the Galaxy RG case, this isn't just a labeling oversight; Salmonella can lead to serious illness, particularly for those with weakened immune systems.

## The Repeat Offenders

With 87 repeat offenders already tallied for the year, some brands are clearly struggling with quality control. Moonlight Packing Corporation leads the pack with a score of 96 and 11 recalls, all Class I. Ghirardelli Chocolate Company follows close behind, tarnishing the reputation of a brand historically associated with sweet indulgence. It's a stark reminder that even the most trusted names can falter.

## What You Can Do

Stay informed and vigilant. Check recall lists regularly and inspect products for any signs of tampering or improper labeling. Remember, the food safety chain isn't perfect, and the burden often falls on the consumer to protect themselves.

## Final Thoughts

While the numbers paint a sobering picture, they also offer a call to action. Each recall is a lesson in what can go wrong and how we can collectively aim to prevent it. Let's hope the industry listens.

Stay safe, and always read the label.
